et&#x000e1;ceos. Campos dos Goytacazes</source><year>2003</year><publisher-name>Master's Dissertation, Universidade Estadual do Norte Fluminense</publisher-name></citation></ref></ref-list><sec sec-type="display-objects"><title>Figures and Tables</title><fig position="float" id="F1"><label>Figure 1</label><caption><p>Northern coast of S&#x000e3;o Paulo State, showing fishers' communities studied (white dots) at S&#x000e3;o Sebasti&#x000e3;o.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="1746-4269-3-9-1"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F2"><label>Figure 2</label><caption><p>Fishers of Enseada beach, taking a gillnet out of their canoe (S&#x000e3;o Sebasti&#x000e3;o, Brasil).</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="1746-4269-3-9-2"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F3"><label>Figure 3</label><caption><p>Whales' and dolphins' classification according to fishers (n = 70) from S&#x000e3;o Sebasti&#x000e3;o, Brazil.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="1746-4269-3-9-3"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F4"><label>Figure 4</label><caption><p>Cetaceans' classification, according to fishers from S&#x000e3;o Sebasti&#x000e3;o, Brazil. Blue circles correspond to biological species, red circles/ellipses are folk species and black ellipses correspond to generic rank.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="1746-4269-3-9-4"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F5"><label>Figure 5</label><caption><p>Atlantic spotted dolphins swimming near to the Vit&#x000f3;ria Island, Ilhabela, Brazil. Note the spotted pattern on the ventral side of the animal. (Photos by 'Projeto SOS Mam&#x000ed;feros Marinhos')</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="1746-4269-3-9-5"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F6"><label>Figure 6</label><caption><p>Groups of cetaceans' species formed by fishers, according to morphological similarities. (G1 = <italic>E. australis </italic>+ <italic>M. novaeangliae</italic>, G2 = <italic>E. australis </italic>+ <italic>M. novaeangliae </italic>+ <italic>O. orca</italic>, G3 = <italic>B. acutorostrata </italic>+ <italic>M. novaeangliae</italic>, G4 = <italic>B. edeni </italic>+ <italic>B. acutorostrata</italic>, G5 = <italic>B. edeni </italic>+ <italic>B. acutorostrata </italic>+ <italic>O. orca </italic>+ <italic>S. bredanensis</italic>, G6 = <italic>S. guianensis </italic>+ <italic>T. truncatus</italic>, G7 = <italic>S. guianensis </italic>+ <italic>T. truncatus </italic>+ <italic>Delphinus sp</italic>., G8 = <italic>S. guianensis </italic>+ <italic>T. truncatus </italic>+ <italic>P. blainvillei</italic>, G9 = <italic>S. guianensis </italic>+ <italic>T. truncatus </italic>+ <italic>S. frontalis</italic>, G10 = <italic>S. guianensis </italic>+ <italic>Delphinus sp</italic>., G11 = <italic>P. blainvillei </italic>+ <italic>Delphinus sp</italic>., G12 = <italic>S. bredanensis </italic>+ <italic>Delphinus sp</italic>., G13 = <italic>S. frontalis </italic>+ <italic>Delphinus sp</italic>., G14 = <italic>S. bredanensis </italic>+ <italic>S. frontalis</italic>, G15 = <italic>S. bredanensis </italic>+ <italic>T. truncatus</italic>, G16 = <italic>S. guianensis </italic>+ <italic>P. blainvillei</italic>, G17 = <italic>P. blainvillei </italic>+ <italic>S. bredanensis</italic>, G18 = <italic>P. blainvillei </italic>+ <italic>T. truncatus</italic>, NG = do not group)</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="1746-4269-3-9-6"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F7"><label>Figure 7</label><caption><p>A female of <italic>Pontoporia blainvillei </italic>(top) and a male of <italic>Sotalia guianensis </italic>(bottom) incidentally caught by gillnets at S&#x000e3;o Sebasti&#x000e3;o, Brazil. (Photos by 'Projeto SOS Mam&#x000ed;feros Marinhos')</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="1746-4269-3-9-7"/></fig><table-wrap position="float" id="T1"><label>Table 1</label><caption><p>Contingency table using the number of citations for life-form categories quoted for whales and dolphins.</p></caption><table frame="hsides" rules="groups"><thead><tr><td align="left">Life-form Categories</td><td align="center">Whales</td><td align="center">Dolphins</td><td align="center">&#x003c7;<sup>2</sup></td><td align="center"><italic>P</italic></td></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td align="left">Fish</td><td align="center">26</td><td align="center">31</td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td align="left">Not Fish</td><td align="center">14</td><td align="center">13</td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td align="left">Mammal</td><td align="center">20</td><td align="center">15</td><td align="center">1.182</td><td align="center">0.554*</td></tr></tbody></table><table-wrap-foot><p>Notes: Ho: proportion of citations for 'Fish', 'Not Fish' and 'Mammal' categories are independent if the animal is whale or dolphin.</p><p>* Non-significant <italic>P </italic>value, Ho is not rejected.</p></table-wrap-foot></table-wrap><table-wrap position="float" id="T2"><label>Table 2</label><caption><p>Cetaceans' names given by the fishers interviewed (n = 70).</p></caption><table frame="hsides" rules="groups"><thead><tr><td align="left"><bold>Scientific name</bold></td><td align="center"><bold>English common name</bold></td><td align="center"><bold>Folk genera</bold></td><td align="center"><bold>Folk species</bold></td><td align="center"><bold>Number of citations</bold></td><td align="center"><bold>Fisher do not know the animal</bold></td><td align="center" colspan="2"><bold>Number of fishers who have seen the animal (S) or have not seen the animal (NS)</bold></td></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td align="left"><italic>Eubalaena australis</italic></td><td align="center">Southern right whale</td><td align="center"><bold>baleia</bold></td><td></td><td align="center"><bold>37</bold></td><td align="center">33</td><td align="center">S = 37</td><td align="center">NS = 33</td></tr><tr><td align="left"><italic>Balaenoptera acutorostrata</italic></td><td align="center">Minke whale</td><td align="center"><bold>baleia</bold></td><td></td><td align="center"><bold>1</bold></td><td align="center">69</td><td align="center">S = 1</td><td align="center">N